  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1035 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nA large UL ridge is covering much of the CONUS with its center\njust off the Gulf Coast of TX and LA. This pattern has steered a\nrich moisture plume our way with the 00Z EPZ sounding showing\n1.56\" of PW. Lack of UL forcing (possibly even a little bit of\nbackground subsidence) and morning cloud coverage did a good job\nof limiting convective coverage this afternoon, especially along \nand east of the Rio Grande. Satellite imagery hints at a weak \ndisturbance over Northern Chihuahua, which will slowly lift north \novernight tonight and into the morning. Guidance is not \nparticularly helpful on whether this disturbance will bring \nshowers and storms north of the border. The HRRR has given a \ndifferent solution with every iteration. Nevertheless, it will \nbring clouds, and that may work to hinder our storm chances again \nfor Saturday. Regardless, our pattern looks to be largely the same\notherwise, very moist (~1.5\" PW) with the westward extension of \nthe ridge overhead, so expect a forecast similar to today with \nscattered mountain storms and isolated to scattered coverage in \nthe lowlands, largely depending on how much cloud/rain lingers \ninto the morning. Heavy rain will be the main concern.\n\nBy Sunday we begin a slow drying trend as the subtropical ridge's\ninfluence increases over the CWA. PW values will drop to around\n1.25\" with H500 temps around -4 degrees C. Convective coverage\nwill drop, especially for the lowlands, but everyone will continue\nto see at least modest rain/storm chances with mountains and\nwestern lowlands favored, and this pattern will hold steady\nthrough at least the middle of next week. The UL high will \ncontinue to extend its influence into NM with the healthiest \nmoisture plume west of the Continental Divide. As we get into \nWednesday and beyond the GFS and Euro diverge with the Euro \nbuilding the ridge over the Desert SW with moisture trapped \nbeneath it while the GFS sends the ridge well to our east with a \ntrough influencing our weather. The Euro would continue this \npattern of scattered mountain and isolated lowland storms while \nthe GFS would bring widespread rain/storm chances. Looking at the \nLREF 500 mb heights, I'd say the truth will lie somewhere in the \nmiddle though probably more ridgy than troughy (not a real word).\n\nTemperatures throughout the period look to more or less hold\nsteady, running about 5 degrees above normal, which is upper the\n80s to near 90 this time of year for the lowlands.",
